Every type trait of the C++ standard library, one per header, built on compiler builtins
(`__is_same`, `__is_trivially_copyable`, ...) instead of `<type_traits>`. The headers are
written by `tooling/gen_traits.py` and not edited by hand.

The std names map to ca names by one rule each:

| std | ca | Kind |
|---|---|---|
| `std::is_x_v<T>` (yes or no) | `ca::is_x<T>` | concept |
| `std::conjunction_v`, `disjunction_v`, `negation_v` | `ca::conjunction`, ... | concept |
| `std::x_t<T>` (a type) | `ca::x<T>` | alias |
| `std::x_v<T>` (a number) | `ca::x<T>` | variable |
| `std::integral_constant`, `true_type`, ... | same name | class |

Since the predicates are concepts, they constrain templates directly and compose with `&&`,
`||` and `!` in requires-clauses, with subsumption between overloads:

```c++
#include "ca/traits.hpp"

template <typename T>
requires ca::is_integral<T> && (!ca::is_same<T, bool>)
[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto twice(const T value) noexcept -> T {
	return static_cast<T>(value * 2);
}

template <ca::is_floating_point T>
[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto twice(const T value) noexcept -> T {
	return value + value;
}

static_assert(twice(21) == 42);
static_assert(twice(1.5) == 3.0);

using text = const char (&)[6];
static_assert(ca::is_same<ca::remove_cvref<text>, char[6]>);
static_assert(ca::is_same<ca::decay<text>, const char*>);
static_assert(ca::extent<char[6]> == 6U);
static_assert(ca::is_same<ca::conditional<(sizeof(long) == 8U), long, long long>, long>);
static_assert(ca::is_trivially_copyable<int> && !ca::is_trivially_copyable<int&>);
static_assert(ca::is_constructible<long, int> && !ca::is_convertible<int*, long>);
```

A predicate used as a value, outside a requires-clause, is a bool expression: `if constexpr
(ca::is_trivially_destructible<T>)` is the idiom around destructor loops. Code that needs a
trait as a type uses `ca::bool_constant<ca::is_x<T>>`.

`is_constant_evaluated()` and the C++26 `is_within_lifetime(p)` are here too, as functions;
`invoke_result` and the `is_invocable` family cover callables, member pointers included.
The deprecated `aligned_storage`, `aligned_union` and `is_pod` are kept for parity with std.
